index 37817c1..71b2747 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- Flash Next on CUDA can read the NVFP4 n-gram table (28.7 GiB, the NVIDIA FP4 checkpoint) with positional reads instead of page faults on its mapping: `TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_READ=pread` reads each row's 80 code bytes and 10 scale bytes with `pread` (O_DIRECT where the file system allows, so the page cache is left to the weights) on up to 128 parked threads (`TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_THREADS`) into a direct-mapped cache of raw rows (`TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_CACHE_MIB`, 256 MiB of address space, resident only where used). The rows are the mapping's bytes, so the dequantized rows and every reply are unchanged. With it, `TF_FLASHNEXT_DRAFT_AHEAD=1` asks for a drafted token's rows while the head drafts the next one, and `TF_FLASHNEXT_WARM_AHEAD=1` reads the next prompt chunk's rows while the current chunk runs. `TF_FLASHNEXT_PREFILL_TAIL=512` evens out a prompt's last chunk with the one before it when it would be under 512 rows, so no chunk takes the decode-style expert kernel. `TF_FLASHNEXT_GATHER_LOG=1` also logs each prompt chunk's gather and the cache's hits.
+
 - Flash Next on CUDA runs the routed NVFP4 experts of prompt chunks of 1,024 rows or more (the NVIDIA FP4 checkpoint) on shared-memory row tiles of 64 pairs, `nvfp4_experts_tile.cu`, with the decode kernel's bytes: a pair's sums run in the same order, so output does not depend on the kernel or the plan's tile. `TF_FLASHNEXT_PROMPT_EXPERT_TILE=2` (the default at one rank) takes gate/up and down, `1` gate/up only, `0` keeps the decode kernel on 16-pair items; `_CFG=pairs,gate_up,down` and `_ROWS` tune it. `tf-cuda-test nvfp4-experts-tile` compares the bytes and times a layer on a GPU. `tf-cuda-test` and `install` build again: `w4a8_tests.zig` passes the quantizer flag `Kernels.prompt` takes, and `contract_controls.cu` includes `act_round.cuh` before it uses it.
 
 ## 1.1.4
